<p>
<xref ref="fig-pd-two-tangents-video"/> builds both pictures up one piece
at a time: the surface on its own, then the plane <m>y = y_0</m> slicing it,
the parabola that slice cuts out, and the tangent line to that parabola at
<m>P</m>, whose slope is <m>\partial f/\partial x</m>. The surface then
returns on its own and the same three steps are repeated in the plane
<m>x = x_0</m>, giving <m>\partial f/\partial y</m>, and the video closes
with both traces and both tangent lines in place.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-pd-two-tangents-video">
<caption>
The two partial derivatives at <m>P</m>, built one at a time: the cut by
<m>y = y_0</m> with its green parabola <m>z = f(x,y_0)</m> and blue tangent
line of slope <m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}</m>, then the cut by
<m>x = x_0</m> with its amber parabola <m>z = f(x_0,y)</m> and red tangent
line of slope <m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m>, and finally the two
together.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-pd-two-tangents"
       source="videos/pd_two_tangents_manim.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/pd_two_tangents_manim.jpg"
       width="100%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<p>
To calculate the partial derivative of <m>f</m> with respect to <m>x</m>,
we treat <m>y</m> as a constant and differentiate normally with respect
to <m>x</m>, i.e.
<md xml:id="eq-pd-as-ordinary-x" number="yes">
\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}
= \left.\frac{d}{dx} f(x,y_0)\right|_{x=x_0}.
</md>
Similarly, to calculate the partial derivative of <m>f</m> with respect
to <m>y</m>, we treat <m>x</m> as a constant and differentiate normally
with respect to <m>y</m>, i.e.
<md xml:id="eq-pd-as-ordinary-y" number="yes">
\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}
= \left.\frac{d}{dy} f(x_0,y)\right|_{y=y_0}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Here are some common notations for the partial derivative with respect to
<m>x</m>:
<md>
\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}
\qquad
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(x_0,y_0)
\qquad
f_x(x_0,y_0).
</md>
Note that similar notation can be used for the partial derivative with
respect to any variable such as <m>y</m> and <m>z</m>.
</p>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-pd-examples">
<title>Examples</title>

<example xml:id="example-pd-paraboloid">
<title>Partial Derivatives of <m>1-x^2-y^2</m></title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = 1 - x^2 - y^2</m> and evaluate the
following partial derivatives at the point
<m>(x_0,y_0) = (1/2, 1/2)</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p><m>f_x</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>f_y</m></p></li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<alert>A.</alert> We first work straight from the definition
<xref ref="eq-pd-def-x"/>, which freezes <m>y</m> at <m>y_0 = 1/2</m> and
moves <m>x</m> from <m>1/2</m> to <m>1/2 + h</m>. The numerator of the
difference quotient is
<md>
<mrow>f\left(\tfrac12+h,\ \tfrac12\right) - f\left(\tfrac12,\tfrac12\right)
  \amp= \left[1 - \left(\tfrac12+h\right)^2 - \left(\tfrac12\right)^2\right]</mrow>
<mrow>\amp\qquad - \left[1 - \left(\tfrac12\right)^2 - \left(\tfrac12\right)^2\right]</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \left(\tfrac12\right)^2 - \left(\tfrac12+h\right)^2</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \tfrac14 - \left(\tfrac14 + h + h^2\right) = -h - h^2,</mrow>
</md>
so that
<md>
\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right|_{(1/2,1/2)}
= \lim_{h\to 0} \frac{-h-h^2}{h}
= \lim_{h\to 0}\, (-1 - h) = -1.
</md>
</p>
<p>
<alert>B.</alert> The definition <xref ref="eq-pd-def-y"/> works the same
way, this time freezing <m>x</m> at <m>x_0 = 1/2</m> and moving <m>y</m>
from <m>1/2</m> to <m>1/2 + h</m>:
<md>
<mrow>f\left(\tfrac12,\ \tfrac12+h\right) - f\left(\tfrac12,\tfrac12\right)
  \amp= \left[1 - \left(\tfrac12\right)^2 - \left(\tfrac12+h\right)^2\right]</mrow>
<mrow>\amp\qquad - \left[1 - \left(\tfrac12\right)^2 - \left(\tfrac12\right)^2\right]</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \left(\tfrac12\right)^2 - \left(\tfrac12+h\right)^2 = -h - h^2,</mrow>
</md>
and therefore
<md>
\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right|_{(1/2,1/2)}
= \lim_{h\to 0} \frac{-h-h^2}{h} = -1.
</md>
</p>
<p>
<alert>The shortcut.</alert> Neither limit had to be computed. By
<xref ref="eq-pd-as-ordinary-x"/>, treating <m>y</m> as a constant gives
<m>f_x = -2x</m>, and by <xref ref="eq-pd-as-ordinary-y"/>, treating
<m>x</m> as a constant gives <m>f_y = -2y</m>. Evaluating at
<m>(1/2,1/2)</m>,
<md>
f_x\left(\tfrac12,\tfrac12\right) = -2\left(\tfrac12\right) = -1,
\qquad
f_y\left(\tfrac12,\tfrac12\right) = -2\left(\tfrac12\right) = -1,
</md>
which is what the two limits above gave.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-pd-quotient">
<title>A Quotient with a Trigonometric Term</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = \dfrac{2y}{y + \cos x}</m> and evaluate
the following partial derivatives at the point
<m>(x_0,y_0) = (0,0)</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p><m>f_x</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>f_y</m></p></li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<alert>A.</alert> Treating <m>y</m> as a constant, we have
<md>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}
= (2y)\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(\frac{1}{y+\cos x}\right)
= (2y)\left(\frac{\sin x}{(y+\cos x)^2}\right)
= \frac{2y \sin x}{(y+\cos x)^2},
</md>
and therefore <m>f_x(0,0) = \dfrac{0}{1} = 0</m>.
</p>
<p>
<alert>B.</alert> Treating <m>x</m> as a constant, we have
<md>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}
= \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(\frac{2y}{y+\cos x}\right)
= \frac{2(y+\cos x) - 2y}{(y+\cos x)^2}
= \frac{2\cos x}{(y+\cos x)^2},
</md>
and therefore <m>f_y(0,0) = \dfrac{2}{1} = 2</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-pd-elliptic">
<title>Partial Derivatives as Slopes on a Surface</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the elliptic paraboloid <m>z = f(x,y) = 4 - x^2 - 2y^2</m>.
Evaluate <m>f_x(1,1)</m> and <m>f_y(1,1)</m> and explain what these
numbers represent.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
We compute <m>f_x(x,y) = -2x</m>, so
<md>
f_x(1,1) = -2.
</md>
Note that the intersection of the surface
<m>z = f(x,y) = 4 - x^2 - 2y^2</m> and the plane <m>y = 1</m> is the
parabola <m>z = 2 - x^2</m>, and <m>f_x(1,1) = -2</m> is the slope of the
tangent line to the curve <m>z = 2 - x^2</m> located in the <m>y = 1</m>
plane. This is the curve <m>C_1</m> in
<xref ref="fig-pd-elliptic-x"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-pd-elliptic-x">
<caption>
The trace <m>C_1</m> cut from <m>z = 4 - x^2 - 2y^2</m> by the plane
<m>y = 1</m> is the parabola <m>z = 2 - x^2</m>. Its tangent line at
<m>(1,1,1)</m> has slope <m>f_x(1,1) = -2</m>.
</caption>
<image width="92%">
<description>
<p>
A tall dome shaped surface cut by the vertical plane y equals one. The
curve of intersection is drawn in dark blue and labelled C one, and a
magenta tangent line touches it at the point 1, 1, 1. A two-line legend
across the top names the surface, the cutting plane, the curve, the
tangent line and the point.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\fontfamily{phv}\selectfont]
\begin{axis}[
  width=10cm, height=9cm,
  view={-40}{16},
  xmin=-2.1, xmax=2.1, ymin=-1.6, ymax=1.6, zmin=0, zmax=5.7,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  ticks=none, clip=false,
  legend cell align=left,
  legend columns=3,
  legend style={
    at={(0.5,1.0)}, anchor=north,
    draw=gray!50, fill=white, fill opacity=0.92, text opacity=1,
    inner sep=5pt, row sep=2pt,
    /tikz/every even column/.append style={column sep=7pt},
    font=\fontfamily{phv}\selectfont,
  },
]
% the surface z = 4 - x^2 - 2y^2 (only where z >= 0)
% parametrised over the elliptical footprint x^2 + 2y^2 <= 4, so the rim
% at z = 0 comes out smooth instead of stair-stepped
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=cyan!25, draw=cyan!55!black,
          fill opacity=0.42, draw opacity=0.35, forget plot,
          z buffer=sort,
          domain=0:1, y domain=0:360, samples=11, samples y=49]
  ({2*x*cos(y)}, {1.4142135624*x*sin(y)}, {4 - 4*x^2});
% the cutting plane y = 1
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, samples=2, samples y=2, domain=-2:2, y domain=0:3.6,
          fill=cyan!45, fill opacity=0.22, draw=cyan!50!black,
          draw opacity=0.35, forget plot]
  ({x},{1},{y});
% the trace C1: z = 2 - x^2 in the plane y = 1
\addplot3[very thick, blue!55!black, forget plot,
          domain=-1.41:1.41, samples=33, samples y=0]
  ({x},{1},{2 - x^2});
% tangent line at (1,1,1), slope -2 in the x-direction
\addplot3[very thick, magenta, forget plot,
          domain=-0.85:0.85, samples=2, samples y=0]
  ({1+x},{1},{1 - 2*x});
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.8, color=magenta, forget plot]
  coordinates {(1,1,1)};
\addlegendimage{area legend, fill=cyan!25, draw=cyan!55!black,
                fill opacity=0.42, draw opacity=0.35}
\addlegendentry{$z=4-x^2-2y^2$}
\addlegendimage{area legend, fill=cyan!45, draw=cyan!50!black,
                fill opacity=0.22, draw opacity=0.35}
\addlegendentry{plane $y=1$}
\addlegendimage{very thick, blue!55!black}
\addlegendentry{$C_1\colon z=2-x^2$}
\addlegendimage{very thick, magenta}
\addlegendentry{tangent, slope $-2$}
\addlegendimage{only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.8, color=magenta}
\addlegendentry{$(1,1,1)$}
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Similarly, <m>f_y(x,y) = -4y</m>, so
<md>
f_y(1,1) = -4.
</md>
The intersection of the surface and the plane <m>x = 1</m> is the
parabola <m>z = 3 - 2y^2</m>, and <m>f_y(1,1) = -4</m> is the slope of
the tangent line to that curve, located in the <m>x = 1</m> plane. This
is the curve <m>C_2</m> in <xref ref="fig-pd-elliptic-y"/>. The tangent
line is steeper than the one in <xref ref="fig-pd-elliptic-x"/>, which
matches <m>|-4| \gt |-2|</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-pd-elliptic-y">
<caption>
The trace <m>C_2</m> cut from <m>z = 4 - x^2 - 2y^2</m> by the plane
<m>x = 1</m> is the parabola <m>z = 3 - 2y^2</m>. Its tangent line at
<m>(1,1,1)</m> has slope <m>f_y(1,1) = -4</m>.
</caption>
<image width="92%">
<description>
<p>
The same dome shaped surface, now cut by the vertical plane x equals one.
The curve of intersection is drawn in dark blue and labelled C two, and a
magenta tangent line touches it at the point 1, 1, 1. This tangent line
is noticeably steeper than the one in the previous figure. A two-line
legend across the top names the surface, the cutting plane, the curve,
the tangent line and the point.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\fontfamily{phv}\selectfont]
\begin{axis}[
  width=10cm, height=9cm,
  view={-40}{16},
  xmin=-2.1, xmax=2.1, ymin=-1.6, ymax=1.6, zmin=0, zmax=5.7,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  ticks=none, clip=false,
