\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<xref ref="fig-pd-elliptic-interactive"/> puts the two slices in one
picture that you can cut open yourself: choose a plane, watch the parabola
it leaves on the dome, and see the tangent line to that parabola appear at
<m>P</m>. Showing both at once makes the comparison the numbers already
report &#8212; that the surface falls away twice as fast in the <m>y</m>
direction as it does in the <m>x</m> direction.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-pd-elliptic-interactive">
<caption>
<xref ref="fig-pd-elliptic-x"/> and <xref ref="fig-pd-elliptic-y"/>, to
cut open. The slice <m>y=1</m> leaves the parabola <m>C_1</m> with tangent
slope <m>f_x(1,1)=-2</m> at <m>P</m>; the slice <m>x=1</m> leaves
<m>C_2</m> with tangent slope <m>f_y(1,1)=-4</m>.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-pd-elliptic"
             iframe="pd-elliptic-manim.html" width="100%" aspect="3:2">
<instructions>
<p>
The dome is <m>z = 4-x^2-2y^2</m> and the white point is
<m>P(1,1,1)</m>. Click <q><m>y=1</m></q> or <q><m>x=1</m></q>, or click a
parabola in the picture, to slice the dome with that plane: the parabola
cut out of it is drawn on the surface and the tangent line to that
parabola at <m>P</m> follows, labelled with its slope. Those slopes are
the two partial derivatives, and the legend collects them. <q>Along
<m>y</m></q> and <q>Along <m>x</m></q> turn the picture until the
<m>y=1</m> and <m>x=1</m> slices respectively are seen edge on, at their
true steepness; dragging turns it by hand.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-pd-second-derivatives">
<title>Second Derivatives and the Mixed Derivative Theorem</title>

<p>
We also define second derivatives such as
<md>
\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x \partial y},
</md>
by which we mean that we first differentiate with respect to <m>y</m> and
then with respect to <m>x</m>. We can alternatively denote such a second
derivative by <m>f_{yx}</m>. Likewise
<md>
\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x^2}
</md>
means that we differentiate with respect to <m>x</m> twice, and we can
alternatively denote such a second derivative by <m>f_{xx}</m>.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-pd-second">
<title>Computing Second Partial Derivatives</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = \cos(xy^2)</m> and evaluate the
following partial derivatives.
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p><m>f_{yx}</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>f_{xy}</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>f_{xx}</m></p></li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<alert>A.</alert>
<md>
<mrow>f_{yx} \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}
\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\cos(xy^2)\right)
= \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(-y^2\sin(xy^2)\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -2y\sin(xy^2) - 2xy^3\cos(xy^2)</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
<alert>B.</alert>
<md>
<mrow>f_{xy} \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}
\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\cos(xy^2)\right)
= \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(-2xy\sin(xy^2)\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -2y\sin(xy^2) - 2xy^3\cos(xy^2)</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
<alert>C.</alert>
<md>
f_{xx} = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}
\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\cos(xy^2)\right)
= \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(-y^2\sin(xy^2)\right)
= -y^4\cos(xy^2)
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<theorem xml:id="thm-mixed-derivative">
<title>The Mixed Derivative Theorem (Clairaut's Theorem)</title>
<statement>
<p>
If <m>f(x,y)</m> and its partial derivatives <m>f_x</m>, <m>f_y</m>,
<m>f_{xy}</m>, and <m>f_{yx}</m> are defined throughout an open region
containing a point <m>(a,b)</m> and are all continuous at <m>(a,b)</m>,
then
<md xml:id="eq-mixed-derivative" number="yes">
f_{xy}(a,b) = f_{yx}(a,b).
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
Why should the order of differentiation not matter? The animation below
gives the geometric reason, and no computation with derivative rules is
involved. Start at a point <m>(a,b)</m>, step by <m>h</m> in the
<m>x</m>-direction and by <m>k</m> in the <m>y</m>-direction, and look at
the four heights of the surface above the corners of the resulting
rectangle. The rises along the two edges in the <m>x</m>-direction are
approximately <m>h\,f_x(a,b)</m> and <m>h\,f_x(a,b{+}k)</m>, so their
difference measures how <m>f_x</m> changes as <m>y</m> increases: it is
approximately <m>hk\,\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\!\left(
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right)</m>. Grouping the same four heights
by the two edges in the <m>y</m>-direction instead measures how
<m>f_y</m> changes as <m>x</m> increases, which is approximately
<m>hk\,\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\!\left(
\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right)</m>. But both groupings expand to
exactly the same number,
<md>
\Delta = f(a{+}h,b{+}k) - f(a{+}h,b) - f(a,b{+}k) + f(a,b),
</md>
in which each corner height appears with a <m>+</m> sign on one diagonal
and a <m>-</m> sign on the other, a pattern that treats <m>x</m> and
<m>y</m> identically. So the two mixed partials approximate the same
quantity <m>\Delta/(hk)</m>, and letting <m>h, k \to 0</m> forces
<m>f_{xy}(a,b) = f_{yx}(a,b)</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-pd-mixed-commute-video">
<caption>
Why <m>f_{xy} = f_{yx}</m>: the four heights of the surface over the
corners of a small rectangle with sides <m>h</m> and <m>k</m>. Differencing
along the two <m>x</m>-edges (green, with blue rises) gives approximately
<m>hk\,\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\!\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}
\right)</m>; differencing along the two <m>y</m>-edges (amber, with red
rises) gives approximately <m>hk\,\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\!\left(
\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right)</m>. Both are the same four-corner
sum <m>\Delta</m>, so shrinking the rectangle to the point gives
<m>f_{xy}(a,b) = f_{yx}(a,b)</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-pd-mixed-commute"
       source="videos/mixed_partials_commute_manim.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/mixed_partials_commute_manim.jpg"
       width="100%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<p>
Consider again <m>f(x,y) = \cos(xy^2)</m> from
<xref ref="example-pd-second"/>. We computed <m>f_{xy}</m> and
<m>f_{yx}</m>, and if you compare the two results, you can see that the
order of differentiation does not matter, which clearly follows from the
mixed derivative theorem.
</p>

<p>
The practical value of <xref ref="thm-mixed-derivative"/> is that we may
choose whichever order makes the computation easier, as the next example
shows.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-pd-mixed-shortcut">
<title>Choosing the Convenient Order</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider
<m>f(x,y) = \cos(x^2 y) + \dfrac{e^{(y^2+y+1)}}{y^2+5}</m> and calculate
<m>f_{xy}</m>.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
We notice that the mixed derivative theorem <xref ref="eq-mixed-derivative"/>
applies, so
<m>f_{xy} = f_{yx}</m>, and that it is faster to compute
<m>f_{yx}</m>. The reason is that differentiating with respect to
<m>x</m> first annihilates the entire second term, which depends on
<m>y</m> alone and is unpleasant to differentiate:
<md>
<mrow>f_{xy} = f_{yx} \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(
\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(\cos(x^2 y)
+ \frac{e^{(y^2+y+1)}}{y^2+5}\right)\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(-2xy\sin(x^2y)\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -2x\sin(x^2y) - 2x^3y\cos(x^2y).</mrow>
</md>
Hence
<md>
f_{xy} = f_{yx} = -2x\sin(x^2y) - 2x^3y\cos(x^2y).
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-pd-compatible-partials">
<title>True or False: Prescribed Partial Derivatives</title>
<statement correct="no">
<p>
For every choice of real constants <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, there exists a
function <m>f</m> with continuous second partial derivatives on
<m>\mathbb R^2</m> such that
<md>
<mrow>f_x(x,y) \amp = a\,y e^{xy} + 2x\sin y + 3x^2,</mrow>
<mrow>f_y(x,y) \amp = x e^{xy} + b\,x^2\cos y + 4y^3,</mrow>
</md>
and <m>f(0,0)=0</m>.
</p>
<p>
Justify your answer fully: give a proof if the statement is true, or a
counterexample if it is false.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
<alert>False.</alert> By <xref ref="thm-mixed-derivative"/>, the two mixed
partial derivatives would have to agree at every point:
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial}{\partial y}(f_x)
\amp = a(1+xy)e^{xy} + 2x\cos y,</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{\partial}{\partial x}(f_y)
\amp = (1+xy)e^{xy} + 2b\,x\cos y.</mrow>
</md>
For example, choosing <m>a=b=0</m> makes these derivatives equal to
<m>0</m> and <m>1</m>, respectively, at <m>(0,0)</m>. Thus no such
function exists for that choice of constants.
</p>
<p>
In fact, equality at <m>(0,0)</m> forces <m>a=1</m>. With <m>a=1</m>,
equality at <m>(1,0)</m> then gives <m>3=1+2b</m>, so <m>b=1</m>.
For <m>a=b=1</m>, the function
<md>
f(x,y)=e^{xy}+x^2\sin y+x^3+y^4-1
</md>
has exactly the prescribed partial derivatives and satisfies
<m>f(0,0)=0</m>. Therefore such a function exists only for
<m>a=b=1</m>, rather than for every choice of constants.
</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-pd-compatible-graphs">
<title>True or False: Can These Be Partial Derivatives?</title>
<statement correct="no">
<p>
The two surfaces below can be the graphs of <m>f_x</m> and <m>f_y</m>
for the same function <m>f</m> with continuous second partial derivatives
on a neighborhood of the origin.
</p>
<sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="0%">
<figure xml:id="fig-pd-candidate-fx">
<caption>
Proposed graph of <m>f_x</m>. The blue trace holds <m>x=0</m>.
</caption>
<image width="100%">
<shortdescription>A proposed partial derivative surface whose highlighted blue trace rises as y increases through the origin.</shortdescription>
<description><p>
The vertical axis gives the value of the proposed partial derivative f x.
The highlighted blue trace lies in the plane x equals zero and passes
through the origin. Moving along this trace in the positive y direction
increases the height. Both coordinate axes and the vertical height axis
are labeled; an arrow on the blue trace points toward increasing y.
</p></description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-pd-candidate-fx"><![CDATA[
import graph3;
viewportmargin=(18,18);
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;
size(8.8cm,8.5cm);
currentprojection=orthographic(camera=(5,-7,4.5),up=Z);
currentlight=light(gray(0.8),specularfactor=0.1,(4,-3,6),(-4,-1,3));

// The formula generates the proposed graph; students see only the surface.
triple proposed(pair p) { return (p.x,p.y,p.y+0.5*p.x^2); }
surface s=surface(proposed,(-1.35,-1.35),(1.35,1.35),16,16);
draw(s,surfacepen=rgb("D6E5EC")+opacity(0.32),
     meshpen=gray(0.55)+opacity(0.28)+linewidth(0.3pt));

pen axisPen=gray(0.22)+linewidth(0.8pt);
draw(Label("$x$",1),(-1.65,0,0)--(1.9,0,0),axisPen,Arrow3(size=5));
draw(Label("$y$",1),(0,-1.65,0)--(0,1.9,0),axisPen,Arrow3(size=5));
draw(Label("$f_x$",1),(0,0,-1.8)--(0,0,2.65),axisPen,Arrow3(size=5));
label("$0$",(0,0,0),SW,axisPen);

pen tracePen=rgb("0072B2")+linewidth(2.7pt);
path3 trace=graph(new triple(real t) {return (0,t,t);},-1.35,1.35);
if(settings.outformat != "html") draw(trace,white+linewidth(4.8pt));
draw(trace,tracePen,Arrow3(size=8));
dot((0,0,0),black+linewidth(4pt));
draw((0,1,0)--(0,1,1),gray(0.4)+dashed+linewidth(0.6pt));
dot((0,1,1),tracePen+linewidth(4pt));
label("$1$",(0,1,0),SE,axisPen);
label("$1$",(0,1,1),W,tracePen);
currentpicture.addBox((-1.8,-1.8,-1.9),(2,2,2.9));
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>
<figure xml:id="fig-pd-candidate-fy">
<caption>
Proposed graph of <m>f_y</m>. The orange trace holds <m>y=0</m>.
</caption>
<image width="100%">
<shortdescription>A proposed partial derivative surface whose highlighted orange trace falls as x increases through the origin.</shortdescription>
<description><p>
The vertical axis gives the value of the proposed partial derivative f y.
The highlighted orange trace lies in the plane y equals zero and passes
through the origin. Moving along this trace in the positive x direction
decreases the height. Both coordinate axes and the vertical height axis
are labeled; an arrow on the orange trace points toward increasing x.
</p></description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-pd-candidate-fy"><![CDATA[
import graph3;
viewportmargin=(18,18);
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;
size(8.8cm,8.5cm);
currentprojection=orthographic(camera=(5,-7,4.5),up=Z);
currentlight=light(gray(0.8),specularfactor=0.1,(4,-3,6),(-4,-1,3));

// The formula generates the proposed graph; students see only the surface.
triple proposed(pair p) { return (p.x,p.y,-p.x+0.5*p.y^2); }
surface s=surface(proposed,(-1.35,-1.35),(1.35,1.35),16,16);
draw(s,surfacepen=rgb("F1E0CD")+opacity(0.32),
     meshpen=gray(0.55)+opacity(0.28)+linewidth(0.3pt));

pen axisPen=gray(0.22)+linewidth(0.8pt);
draw(Label("$x$",1),(-1.65,0,0)--(1.9,0,0),axisPen,Arrow3(size=5));
draw(Label("$y$",1),(0,-1.65,0)--(0,1.9,0),axisPen,Arrow3(size=5));
draw(Label("$f_y$",1),(0,0,-1.8)--(0,0,2.65),axisPen,Arrow3(size=5));
label("$0$",(0,0,0),SW,axisPen);

pen tracePen=rgb("D55E00")+linewidth(2.7pt);
path3 trace=graph(new triple(real t) {return (t,0,-t);},-1.35,1.35);
if(settings.outformat != "html") draw(trace,white+linewidth(4.8pt));
draw(trace,tracePen,Arrow3(size=8));
dot((0,0,0),black+linewidth(4pt));
draw((1,0,0)--(1,0,-1),gray(0.4)+dashed+linewidth(0.6pt));
dot((1,0,-1),tracePen+linewidth(4pt));
label("$1$",(1,0,0),NE,axisPen);
label("$-1$",(1,0,-1),S,tracePen);
currentpicture.addBox((-1.8,-1.8,-1.9),(2,2,2.9));
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>
</sidebyside>
<p>
Decide whether the statement is true or false. Justify your answer using
slopes visible in the graphs, without trying to recover a formula for
<m>f</m>. The arrows on the highlighted traces point in the positive
coordinate directions.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
<alert>False.</alert> On the left, hold <m>x=0</m> and increase <m>y</m>.
The blue trace rises through the origin, so
<md>
\frac{\partial}{\partial y}(f_x)(0,0) \gt 0.
</md>
On the right, hold <m>y=0</m> and increase <m>x</m>. The orange trace
falls through the origin, so
<md>
\frac{\partial}{\partial x}(f_y)(0,0) \lt 0.
</md>
These two mixed partial derivatives have opposite signs and cannot be
equal. This contradicts <xref ref="thm-mixed-derivative"/>, which would
apply because the second partial derivatives are continuous near the
origin. Therefore the two proposed graphs cannot come from the same
function <m>f</m>.
</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

</subsection>

</section>
